---
layout: article
title: Send events over REST
description: Send Appwrite Analytics events with a plain HTTP request from any platform, including native iOS and Android apps, games, and command-line tools.
---

Any client that can make an HTTP request can send analytics events. Use REST when your platform has no tracking helper, such as a native iOS or Android app, a game engine, a smart TV app, or a command-line tool.

Each event is one `POST` request to your property. Client-side events need no API key.

# Send an event {% #send-an-event %}

Send the event name and the URL it happened on.

```bash
curl -X POST 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/analytics/properties/<PROPERTY_ID>/events' \
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
    -H 'X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>' \
    -d '{
        "name": "pageview",
        "url": "https://example.com/pricing",
        "referrer": "https://www.google.com/"
    }'
```

Appwrite responds with `204 No Content`.

An event named `pageview` counts as a pageview. Any other name is a custom event. Add `props` to attach details, as a flat list that alternates keys and values.

```bash
curl -X POST 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/analytics/properties/<PROPERTY_ID>/events' \
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
    -H 'X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>' \
    -d '{
        "name": "signup_completed",
        "url": "https://example.com/signup",
        "props": ["plan", "pro", "source", "pricing_page"]
    }'
```

# Parameters {% #parameters %}

| Parameter | Type | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `name` | string | Required. The event name, up to 64 characters. |
| `url` | string | Required. The full URL the event happened on, including the scheme, such as `https://example.com/pricing`. For a native app, use your own scheme, such as `app://ios/settings`. |
| `domain` | string | The hostname to record. Defaults to the hostname of `url`. |
| `referrer` | string | The URL of the page that sent the visitor. |
| `screenWidth` | integer | The width of the window or viewport in CSS or logical pixels. |
| `scrollDepth` | integer | How far the visitor scrolled, from `0` to `100` percent. |
| `engagementTime` | integer | Seconds the visitor was active since you last sent this value. |
| `sessionHash` | string | A value, up to 128 characters, that keeps separate sessions apart when they come from the same IP address and user agent. |
| `props` | string[] | Custom properties as alternating keys and values. Up to 32 items, so 16 key and value pairs, each up to 255 characters. |
| `userId`, `ip`, `userAgent` | string | Server-side only. They need an API key. See [Track events from your server](/docs/products/analytics/server-side). |

Appwrite records the path of `url` without its query string and fragment. Before it drops the query string, it reads UTM parameters and ad click IDs from it, so `https://example.com/?utm_source=newsletter` is recorded as the page `/` with `newsletter` as its UTM source. See [Traffic sources](/docs/products/analytics/sources).

# Who an event belongs to {% #visitors %}

Appwrite counts visitors from the request itself: the IP address it came from, its `User-Agent` header, and the hostname. Send events from the user's device, so every user is counted separately and located by their own IP address.

Appwrite reads the browser, operating system, and device from the `User-Agent` header. When it doesn't recognize the value, those fields stay empty and the event still counts. For how visitors and sessions are counted, see [Visitors and sessions](/docs/products/analytics/visitors).

# Origins {% #origins %}

Browsers add an `Origin` header to these requests, and so does the Appwrite Flutter SDK. When a request has an `Origin` header, its hostname or package name must belong to an app in your project, under **Apps**.

Native HTTP clients usually send no `Origin` header. Appwrite accepts those requests while the property allows every origin, which is the default. If you limit a property's allowed origins, requests without a matching `Origin` header are rejected unless they use an API key with the `analytics.write` scope. See [Properties](/docs/products/analytics/properties).

# Errors {% #errors %}

| Status | Type | Cause |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `400` | `general_argument_invalid` | A parameter is missing or invalid, such as a URL without a scheme or a name over 64 characters. |
| `401` | `general_access_forbidden` | The origin isn't in the property's allowed origins, or the request sets `userId`, `ip`, or `userAgent` without an API key. |
| `403` | `general_unknown_origin` | The `Origin` header doesn't belong to an app in the project. |
| `404` | `analytics_property_not_found` | The property ID is wrong, or the property is turned off. |
